The RenderGI RT algorithm is a Monte Carlo-based path tracing algorithm for rendering, which is widely used in the computer graphics industry. Here is a simplified pseudocode representation of the algorithm, including refraction:
```python
function RenderGI(sentence, samples)
    // Initialize buffers
    pixels[] = CreatePixelBuffer(sentence.width, sentence.height)
    heap[] = CreateVoxelHeap(sentence.width, sentence.height)

    // Main rendering loop
    for sample in range(samples):
        // Select a viewpoint (camera) randomly
        viewpoint = SelectRandomViewpoint(sentence)

        // Perform camera intrinsics transformation
        screenPos = TransformViewpointToScreenPixels(viewpoint, sentence)

        // Convert screen pixels to photon coordinates
        pixelX, pixelY = RoundToNearestInt(screenPos.x, screenPos.y)

        // Get the pixel normal and material
        normal = GetPixelNormal(sentence, pixelX, pixelY)
        material = GetPixelMaterial(sentence, pixelX, pixelY)

        // Compute direct illumination
        directIllumination = ComputeDirectIllumination(viewpoint, normal, material, sentence)

        // Sample a random direction (for indirect illumination)
        direction = SampleRandomDirection()

        // Perform refraction
        if material.isTransparent:
            // Compute refraction indices
            n1 = material.refractiveIndex
            n2 = GetMediumRefractiveIndex(GetIntersectionMedium(normal, direction))

            // Compute refraction coefficient
            coefficient = ComputeRefractionCoefficient(n1, n2)

            // Sample a new direction after refraction
            direction = SampleRefraction(direction, coefficient)

        // Perform indirect illumination
        indirectIllumination = ComputeIndirectIllumination(viewpoint, direction, material, sentence)

        // Add direct and indirect illumination to the pixel
        pixels[pixelX, pixelY] += directIllumination + indirectIllumination

    // Accumulate pixels
    outputImage = AccumulatePixels(pixels, sentence)
    return outputImage
```
